# Screen Editor (SE) — Python Native Linux Port
================================================================================
Install and Start Instructions for Native Python 3 Screen Editor
================================================================================

1. System Requirements & Prerequisites
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Operating System:
  - Linux x86_64 (PC / Workstation)
  - Linux aarch64 (Raspberry Pi 4, Raspberry Pi 5)
- Python Runtime:
  - Python 3.10 or newer (tested on 3.10, 3.11, 3.12+)
- Zero Third-Party Dependencies:
  - Pure Python standard library only (termios, tty, select, fcntl, os, sys)
  - No pip packages or external C extensions required to run
- Terminal Console:
  - ANSI / VT100 compatible terminal emulator (xterm, GNOME Terminal, Kitty,
    Alacritty, Linux VT console, tmux)
  - Standard geometry: 80 columns x 24 rows (or wide 120x40)

2. Installation Instructions
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
There are three convenient ways to install or run the Python SE editor:

Method A: Zero-Install Direct Execution (Recommended for quick use)
  No installation or virtual environment is needed. Run directly from the
  repository:

    chmod +x ports/python/bin/se
    ports/python/bin/se [filename]

  Alternatively, invoke via top-level Makefile:

    make se-py
    make se-py ARGS="myfile.txt"

Method B: User / Virtualenv Package Installation (pip)
  Install the package in editable development mode so the 'se' command is
  globally accessible within your Python environment:

    pip install -e ports/python

  Or install for your current user:

    pip install --user ports/python

  Once installed, the executable binary 'se' will be placed on your PATH.

Method C: User Binary Symlink
  Create a symbolic link in ~/.local/bin or /usr/local/bin:

    ln -s "$(pwd)/ports/python/bin/se" ~/.local/bin/se

3. Start & Launch Instructions
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Command Syntax:
  se [filename[:line]] [-l LINE] [--config PATH] [-v] [-h]

Usage Examples:
  se                     Start editor with empty buffer (Untitled)
  se notes.txt           Open existing file notes.txt (or create on save)
  se src/main.c:45       Open src/main.c and jump directly to line 45
  se -l 100 script.py    Open script.py with cursor positioned at line 100
  se --config SE.CFG     Load custom SE.CFG configuration settings
  se --version           Display version number and platform information
  se --help              Display complete CLI help options

Starting via Makefile:
  make se-py                        Launch empty editor session
  make se-py ARGS="test.txt"        Launch editor with target file

Running Automated Verification Suite:
  make test-py                      Run full unit and PTY test suite
  python3 tests/test_python_port.py Run port tests directly

4. User Interface & Keybinding Reference
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The Python port adheres 100% to docs/UI_UX_DESIGN.md specifications:
- Tier 1 Workspaces: Mathematically centered editor, splash, and help screens
  padded with column 0 tildes (~) on empty rows.
- Tier 2 Boxed Modals: Centered ASCII frames (+---+) for queries and dialogs.
- Universal Status Bar: Fixed bottom row (Row 24 or 40) displaying:
  | FILENAME.EXT* | 80x24 | Linux | ^K: Editor keys | Line 1/1 Col 0 |

Core Keyboard Controls (WordStar & Function Keys):
  Cursor Navigation:
    Arrows / WordStar    ^S (Left), ^D (Right), ^E (Up), ^X (Down)
    Word / Line Jumps    ^A (Line Start / Home), ^E (Line End)
    Document Jumps       ^T (Top of file), ^G (Bottom of file)
    Page Jumps           ^U (Page Up), ^V (Page Down)

  Text Editing Primitives:
    Enter                Split line at cursor (max 120 columns)
    Backspace            Delete character left / join with previous line
    Delete               Delete character under cursor / join next line
    Tab                  Insert spaces aligned to configured tab width (4 or 8)

  Block Operations:
    Ctrl+B               Toggle block selection mode ([SEL] / [BLK LOCK])
    Ctrl+C               Copy selected block to clipboard
    Ctrl+X               Cut selected block to clipboard
    Ctrl+V               Paste clipboard buffer at cursor position

  Search and Replace:
    Ctrl+F               Open Find Text input modal
    Ctrl+N               Find next occurrence of search pattern
    Ctrl+R               Open Search and Replace modal dialog
    Ctrl+L               Goto line number modal

  File Operations & Session:
    Ctrl+S               Save document (prompts if Untitled)
    Ctrl+O               Open file prompt
    Ctrl+Q               Safe quit (prompts to save if buffer modified)
    Ctrl+K               Display full-screen Editor Mode Help workspace
    ESC                  Return to Tier 1 Splash screen menu

  Plugins & Subsystems:
    Ctrl+P               Open context-aware Plugin Palette modal dialog
    Ctrl+Y               Format Z80 source code via BEAUTIFY plugin

5. Configuration Engine (SE.CFG)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
SE automatically checks the current working directory for SE.CFG upon startup.
Supported configuration directives:
  SCREEN=80x24         Terminal geometry (80x24 or 120x40)
  TAB=4                Tab expansion width in spaces (4 or 8)
  LINES=32K            Maximum document buffer capacity (16K or 32K)
  RAMDRIVE=ENABLED     RAM drive status flag (ENABLED or DISABLED)

Save active configuration anytime from the Splash screen menu (:c -> [S]).

6. Plugin Subsystem
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Plugins reside under ports/python/plugins/ and are discovered dynamically.
Built-in plugins include:
  - ROT13    : Rotate alphabetical characters by 13 positions (line or block).
  - WORDCNT  : Calculate line, word, and character counts in Tier 2 modal.
  - BEAUTIFY : Align Z80 assembly labels, opcodes, operands, and comments.

To create a custom plugin, inherit from PluginBase in se.plugin:

  from se.plugin import PluginBase, HostAPI

  class MyPlugin(PluginBase):
      NAME = "MYPLUG"
      DESCRIPTION = "Custom text utility"

      def run(self, api: HostAPI) -> bool:
          line = api.checkout_line(0)
          api.commit_line(0, line.upper())
          api.show_modal("My Plugin", "First line transformed to upper case.")
          return True
